sol (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Signals: attention as light from a place. `sol signal` lets anything on
    the canvas — an agent's finish hook, a wrapped build, a hand — make its
    district call for you: an ember on the name plate, a star on the bar
    chip (amber routine, red urgent), a mark on the map, one line in the
    footer. Nothing slides in, nothing covers your work, and the camera does
    not move: signals call, they never drag.
  * The call is transmitted as a drop into still water: a train of crests
    from the calling planet, each weaker than the last, broadening as they
    travel, from a centre that bobs as it settles — the leading crest at
    one fixed speed, so a call from Neptune visibly takes longer to reach
    you than one from Venus. Urgent calls hit harder and rebound more. It
    is a click-through layer surface (needs python3-gi-cairo), profiled
    rather than guessed at — twice: a full-screen toolkit surface pays a
    flat CPU tax per committed frame whatever is painted, so the frames
    go where the eye is and the surface bows out the moment the leading
    crest leaves the screen. A beat whose wave has not arrived commits
    nothing at all, which is what makes a call from far away nearly free
    until it reaches you.
  * `sol answer` (mod+s) flies to the oldest call, urgent first — and
    arrival is the acknowledgement. Arriving at a district answers its
    calls however you arrive — arriving, not merely being parked there:
    a camera left on Mars overnight does not eat the calls Mars makes
    while you sleep — so there are no dismiss buttons and no unread pile:
    the camera only moves when you move it, which makes the camera
    arriving your attention arriving.
  * `sol signal -- cmd` runs the command and calls by its exit code,
    reading the place when the work starts — the honest moment — and
    exiting with the same code so pipelines cannot tell it was there
    (a job killed by a signal exits 128+N, the way a shell would say it).
    `--at` and SOL_AT attribute calls explicitly for agents' shells.
  * The queue is written atomically under a lock — agents' hooks, the
    watcher's beat and mod+s can all land in the same instant without
    resurrecting or clobbering one another's calls — a message's own
    `&` or `<` cannot blank the footer that reports it, and a bar
    restarted into a recycled pid is never signalled before it listens.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sat, 15 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

sol (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `sol session`: which desktop this machine starts, and a console picker
    at logout for machines without a display manager — the job of a greeter,
    done by the console you were already on. Sessions run in a transient
    systemd scope, and graphical-session.target is stopped after each one,
    so a session that ends is really over, whoever started its pieces.
  * Living beside KDE: sol doctor now names user units enabled into
    graphical-session.target that duplicate sol's own bar and notifier —
    the "extra header" every desktop was getting — and portal backends the
    conf names but the machine lacks. The package Recommends
    xdg-desktop-portal-wlr and -gtk, and install.sh warns when they are
    missing.
  * A logout stays a logout: the picker ends the login rather than
    restarting the desktop when it has no terminal to ask on or nothing to
    choose between, a typo at it keeps what you had, the spin guard counts
    only sessions that die within seconds of starting, and a saved pick no
    longer applies under a display manager, which already asked.
  * Session entries honor TryExec, so a half-uninstalled desktop leaves the
    picker; gdm is no longer accused of needing an X server its greeter
    does not want; driftwm is required only by the sessions that run it.
  * A sky you can tell apart: Venus goes bronze and Neptune indigo so no
    two worlds rhyme, district cards get a drawn edge, the Sun a limb, the
    name plates a size you can read from orbit, the help card its
    hierarchy in colour, and every overlay the same corner. A mode tints
    the resting window border with its planet's colour, and the overview
    shades the sky behind it — and puts it back, even when its terminal is
    killed under it.
  * Flights stop stumbling. The district-card rewrite waits until the
    camera lands, because a shader reload freezes the frame and used to
    land mid-journey; near-equal zooms keep their altitude; a cruise climb
    has to buy a view; and no zoom command is ever issued while the path
    is streaming, because driftwm's zoom tick owns the camera during its
    ease and discards everything it is sent. Flights hold the moving
    stamp, and a flight nobody asked for by name no longer overwrites the
    breadcrumb `sol back` answers with.
  * A district closes over what arrives and what leaves. A window is left
    alone until it has hatched — driftwm announces a toplevel before its
    client commits a buffer — and past a second a thin window is taken at
    its word. Births, deaths and carryings landing in one beat fold in as
    one motion with one card write; what a mode must defer is kept for
    the beat after it ends; a home is honoured mode or no mode; and the
    claims file is written once per pass, not once per district.
  * The camera finishes what a keypress started, and nothing else: the
    view reframes only when you were watching the whole district and a
    console you asked for grew it past the edge — with the keyboard on a
    window of that burst — never for a closure, and never out of a
    reading position you deliberately went into.
  * The Sun holds nothing, everywhere the rule is asked: windows live on
    the eight planets, a window at the origin belongs to Mercury, gather
    goes home, and a stale claim on the origin is an old file, not a
    district.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 12:30:00 +0000

sol (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sol is a desktop you can choose. A wayland-session entry puts it in the
    login screen beside every other desktop on the machine, so switching to
    sol and back is picking a different session rather than booting specially.
  * graphical-session.target is started, through a sol-session.target that
    depends on it — portals, notification daemons and keyring agents are
    wired to that target and were never starting.
  * Portals are told which backend this desktop uses, and told under the
    right name: the value that decides is the one the session manager holds,
    and driftwm sets its own for the processes it spawns.
  * sol doctor reports all three, and reports the desktop name portals will
    actually read rather than the one in the caller's environment.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0000

sol (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * focus: ask the windows, not only the screen. A client declares a minimum
    size and driftwm clamps a Resize to it, so a window asked for a twelfth of
    a screen can commit three times that and sit across its neighbours —
    twelve real applications was twenty-four overlaps. Terminals shrink to
    almost nothing and hid this completely. sol now reads back what was
    actually committed, remembers it against the app_id, and lays out again
    with fewer and larger tiles: one extra pass the first time an application
    is met, and none ever after.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0000

sol (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release.
  * The solar system as a workspace model for driftwm: districts, the bar,
    the overview, the context menu, drag-between-planets and the shader.
  * Screen modes — focus, solo, present — plus the night and dark palettes.
    These need a driftwm whose IPC has a Resize, and say so when it does not.
  * Installed as a module with a stub on the path, so Python caches its
    compiled form rather than re-parsing on every keypress.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000
